# Basic Git Commands

A quick reference for commonly used Git commands.

> Replace placeholders such as `<branch>`, `<file>`, and `<url>` with your values, without the angle brackets.
> Examples use `main`; replace it with `master` or another branch name as needed.

## 1. Initialize and Configure

| Command | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `git init` | Initialize a repository in the current directory. |
| `git init <directory>` | Initialize a repository in the specified directory. |
| `git config --global user.name "Incia"` | Set the author name for commits. |
| `git config --global user.email "incia.anand@gmail.com"` | Set the author email for commits. Use an email associated with your GitHub account for commit attribution. |
| `git clone <url>` | Clone a remote repository locally. |

## 2. Manage Branches

| Command | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `git branch` | List local branches. |
| `git branch -a` | List local and remote-tracking branches. |
| `git checkout -b <branch>` | Create and switch to a new branch. |
| `git checkout <branch>` | Switch to an existing branch. |
| `git branch -d <branch>` | Delete a local branch if Git considers it safely merged. |
| `git branch -D <branch>` | Force-delete a local branch, even if it contains unmerged commits. |

> Switch to another branch before deleting a branch.

### Alternative: `git switch`

```bash
# Create and switch to a new branch
git switch -c branchname-incia

# Switch to an existing branch
git switch branchname-anand
```

## 3. Stage and Commit Changes

| Command | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `git add -A` | Stage all changes across the repository, including new files, modifications, and deletions. |
| `git add <file>` | Stage changes to a specific file. |
| `git commit -m "<message>"` | Commit staged changes. |
| `git commit -a -m "<message>"` | Stage modifications and deletions of tracked files, then commit. Does not include untracked files. |
| `git commit -am "<message>"` | Shorthand for `git commit -a -m "<message>"`. |
| `git commit --signoff -m "<message>"` | Commit staged changes and add a `Signed-off-by` line using your configured identity. |

> `--signoff` adds a sign-off statement; it does not cryptographically sign the commit.

## 4. Inspect Changes and History

| Command | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `git status` | Show the current branch, staged changes, unstaged changes, and untracked files. |
| `git diff` | Show unstaged changes in tracked files compared with the staging area. |
| `git diff --staged` | Show staged changes compared with the latest commit. |
| `git diff <commit-1> <commit-2>` | Compare two commits. |
| `git diff <branch-1>..<branch-2>` | Compare the file contents at the tips of two branches. |
| `git log` | Show commit history for the current branch. |
| `git log --oneline` | Show a compact commit history. |
| `git grep -F "func()"` | Search tracked files in the working directory for the literal text `func()`. |

## 5. Manage Remote Repositories

| Command | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `git remote add <name> <url>` | Add a remote repository. |
| `git remote` | List remote names. |
| `git remote -v` | List remote names and their URLs. |
| `git remote remove <name>` | Remove a remote configuration from the local repository. |
| `git remote rename <old-name> <new-name>` | Rename a remote. |

### Example

```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/username/repository.git
```

## 6. Fetch, Pull, and Push

| Command | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `git fetch <remote>` | Download remote updates without integrating them into the current branch. |
| `git fetch <remote> <branch>` | Fetch a specific branch from a remote. |
| `git pull` | Fetch and integrate changes from the current branch's configured upstream. |
| `git pull <remote>` | Fetch from the remote and integrate the branch selected by Git's configuration. |
| `git pull <remote> <branch>` | Fetch the specified remote branch and integrate it into the current branch. |
| `git push <remote> <branch>` | Push the specified local branch to the remote. |
| `git push -u origin <branch>` | Push a branch and set its upstream for future pushes and pulls. |
| `git push origin main` | Push the local `main` branch to `origin`. |

> Pulling integrates changes using merge or rebase, depending on your options and configuration. It may require you to choose a strategy when branches have diverged.

## 7. Undo Changes

| Command | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `git reset <commit>` | Move the current branch to the specified commit and reset the staging area, while keeping working-directory contents. |
| `git reset --soft <commit>` | Move the current branch to the specified commit while keeping the staging area and working-directory contents unchanged. |
| `git reset --hard <commit>` | Move the current branch to the specified commit and reset both the staging area and working directory to match it. |
| `git reset -- <file>` | Unstage a file while keeping its working-directory contents. |
| `git restore --staged <file>` | Another way to unstage a file. |
| `git revert <commit>` | Create a new commit that reverses the changes introduced by a specified commit. |

> **Caution:** `git reset --hard` discards uncommitted tracked-file changes and may overwrite untracked files that are in the way. Use `git revert` when undoing commits on shared branches to preserve history.

## 8. Merge Branches

Run these commands from the branch that should receive the changes.

| Command | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `git merge <branch>` | Merge the specified branch into the current branch. |
| `git merge --no-ff <branch>` | Create a merge commit even when a fast-forward merge is possible. |
| `git merge --squash <branch>` | Prepare the combined changes from a branch in the staging area without creating a commit. |

### Squash Merge Example

```bash
git switch main
git merge --squash branchname-incia
git commit -m "Add completed feature"
```

## 9. Remove, Move, and Rename Files

| Command | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `git rm <file>` | Remove a tracked file from the working directory and stage its deletion. |
| `git rm --cached <file>` | Remove a file from Git's index while keeping the local file. After committing, Git stops tracking it. |
| `git mv <current-path> <new-path>` | Move or rename a tracked file and stage the change. |

> To keep a file untracked after using `git rm --cached`, add an appropriate entry to `.gitignore`.

## 10. Stash Changes

| Command | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `git stash` | Temporarily save staged and unstaged changes to tracked files, then restore them to the current commit's state. |
| `git stash push -m "<message>"` | Create a stash with a descriptive message. |
| `git stash push -u -m "<message>"` | Stash tracked changes and untracked files, excluding ignored files. |
| `git stash list` | List saved stashes. |
| `git stash apply 'stash@{0}'` | Apply a stash without removing it from the stash list. |
| `git stash pop 'stash@{0}'` | Apply a stash and remove it if it applies successfully. |
| `git stash drop 'stash@{0}'` | Delete a specific stash. |
| `git stash clear` | Delete all stashes. |
| `git stash show 'stash@{0}'` | Show a summary of changes in a stash. |
| `git stash show -p 'stash@{0}'` | Show the patch for a stash. |

> `stash@{0}` is the most recent stash. Use `git stash list` to find other entries.
> `git stash push -m` is preferred over the older `git stash save` command.
> Dropping or clearing stashes removes saved changes; check them first.

## 11. Example Workflow

```bash
# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/username/repository.git
cd repository

# Create a feature branch
git switch -c feature/my-feature

# Make changes, then inspect them
git status
git diff

# Stage and review changes
git add -A
git diff --staged

# Commit changes
git commit -m "Add my feature"

# Push the branch and set its upstream
git push -u origin feature/my-feature
```

After pushing, open a pull request on GitHub to propose merging your changes.
